    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, i am studying phonetics and im a bit confused. can u explain the difference between voiced and unvoiced sounds? specifically for the alveolar fricatives like /s/ and /z/? i dont really get how the vocal cords work in it.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Voiced and unvoiced sounds, like /s/ and /z/, are determined by whether your vocal cords vibrate when you make the sound. For the unvoiced /s/, you push air through a narrow gap, but your vocal cords remain relaxed and don't buzz. In contrast, for the voiced /z/, you do the same thing with the air stream, but your vocal cords vibrate, creating that characteristic buzzing sound. Think of it like this: /s/ is just air escaping, while /z/ is air escaping *with* a little internal rumble from your vocal cords.",
      "source": "target",
