    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. the buzzing is what gets me though, sometimes it just feels like a weird, scratchy noise in my throat rather than a clean hum. are there other sounds where that kind of \"noisy\" or \"scratchy\" quality happens because of the way the air moves?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, that \"noisy\" or \"scratchy\" quality is the hallmark of fricatives, which is where the air is forced through a narrow constriction. The alveolar fricatives like /s/ and /z/ are just one example of this air turbulence. You'll find similar noisy sounds in other places, like the voiceless postalveolar fricative /ʃ/ (as in \"ship\") or the voiced postalveolar fricative /ʒ/ (as in \"measure\"). These sounds are created by creating a more complex turbulence pattern in the airflow.",
      "source": "target",
